%[text] %[text:anchor:T_3AA44D56] # Lab 2: Complex Fourier Series
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%%
%[text] **1.** Rewrite the complex function 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(t) = 3 \\cos (4 \\pi t) + 3 i \\sin (4 \\pi t)${"altText":"g(t) = 3 \cos (4 \pi t) + 3 i \sin (4 \pi t)"} 
%[text] as a complex exponential 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(t) = A e^{i \\omega t}${"altText":"g(t) = A e^{i \omega t}"}.
%[text] What are the magnitude $A${"altText":"A"} and angular frequency $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"} (in rad/s)? Assume $t${"altText":"t"} has units of seconds.
% Replace the NaNs with your answers 
A = 3
omega = 4*pi
%%
%[text] **2.** What are the complex coefficients $c\_{1}${"altText":"c\_{1}"} and $c\_2${"altText":"c\_2"} that generate the pictured complex function? The other coefficients are zero.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:85cc)
%[text] Use the sliders in the [complex Fourier series](<matlab: run ComplexFourierSeriesApp>) app to identify the coefficients. Round to the nearest half. Your coefficients should be given in the form $a + bi${"altText":"a + bi"}.
% Replace the NaNs with your answers in the form a + bi
c1 = 0.5
c2 = 0.5 - 0.5i
%%
%[text] **3.** Consider the real function
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(t) = 2 \\sin( 4 \\pi t ) + 2 \\cos (4 \\pi t)${"altText":"f(t) = 2 \sin( 4 \pi t ) + 2 \cos (4 \pi t)"} 
%[text] This function can be represented by a complex Fourier series of the form
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(t) = \\sum\_{-\\infty}^\\infty c\_n e^{ 2 \\pi n i t}${"altText":"f(t) = \sum\_{-\infty}^\infty c\_n e^{ 2 \pi n i t}"} 
%[text] In this form, $c\_2 = 1-i${"altText":"c\_2 = 1-i"}. What is the value of $c\_{-2}${"altText":"c\_{-2}"}?
% Replace NaN with the value of c_{-2} 
cm2 = 1 + 1i
%%
%[text] **4.** What is the magnitude and phase (in degrees) of the coefficient $c\_{-2}${"altText":"c\_{-2}"} that you solved for in question **3**?
% Replace the NaNs with your answers
mag_cm2 = abs(cm2)
phase_cm2 = angle(cm2)*180/pi
%%
%[text] **5**. What is the amplitude of the function $f(t)${"altText":"f(t)"} that you solved for in question **3**?
magf = sqrt(8)
%%
%[text] **6.** How is the magnitude of the coefficient $c\_{-2}${"altText":"c\_{-2}"} related to the overall magnitude of the real function $f${"altText":"f"}?
% Write a brief answer in the quotes
relation = "|f| = 2|c_{-2}|"
%%
%[text] **7.** What are the complex coefficients $c\_{-3}${"altText":"c\_{-3}"} and $c\_3${"altText":"c\_3"} that generate the pictured real function $g${"altText":"g"}? The other coefficients are zero.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:2a72)
%[text] Use the sliders in the [complex Fourier series](<matlab: run ComplexFourierSeriesApp>) app to identify the coefficients. Round to the nearest quarter (0.25). Your coefficients should be given in the form $a + bi${"altText":"a + bi"}.
% Replace the NaNs with your answers in the form a + bi
cm3 = -0.25 - 0.5i
c3 = -0.25 + 0.5i
%%
%[text] **8.** What is the magnitude of the real function $g${"altText":"g"} that you created in question **7**? Your answer should be computed from the coefficients, but you can verify your result by examining the plot.
magg = sqrt(1.25)
%[text] 
%%
